record with Outside the Lines

00:06:00.781 --> 00:06:03.045
saying that he believes
Spygate was really bad,

00:06:03.045 --> 00:06:04.043
and that,

00:06:04.043 --> 00:06:05.378
- They lost the Superbowl because of it.

00:06:05.378 --> 00:06:06.318
- He didn't say that.

00:06:06.318 --> 00:06:07.723
- I'm saying it.

00:06:07.723 --> 00:06:10.080
- But he's saying Goodell asked
him to put out the statement

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.780
and he didn't believe it,
saying that he put out.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:13.975
Outside the Lines, huge
story, exploding right now.

00:06:13.975 --> 00:06:15.612
- I love it.

00:06:15.612 --> 00:06:17.759
We hate the Patriots around here.

00:06:17.759 --> 00:06:19.930
And that's just a bottom line.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:20.484
Wow.

00:06:20.484 --> 00:06:22.109
- [Steve] That's good, I got
a question about Deflategate.

00:06:22.109 --> 00:06:23.421
- You know what, is that right?

00:06:23.421 --> 00:06:25.127
That being said, I do
wanna say the following.

00:06:25.127 --> 00:06:27.588
I'm not upset about the Tom Brady ruling,

00:06:27.588 --> 00:06:29.690
a lot of you asked me, I'm
happy that he's playing.

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:30.561
I'm happy.

00:06:30.561 --> 00:06:33.272
Alright, I'm serious, by the way.

00:06:33.272 --> 00:06:34.404
I'd rather him play.

00:06:34.404 --> 00:06:36.232
We don't play them in those sport games.

00:06:37.306 --> 00:06:38.327
- [Steve] Interviewee questions.

00:06:38.327 --> 00:06:39.401
- Oh, interviewee.

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:44.422
Yeah, you know, the truth is,

00:06:44.422 --> 00:06:45.827
I don't have a really
great answer for this.

00:06:46.797 --> 00:06:49.878
I like when interviewees
ask me honest questions.

00:06:49.878 --> 00:06:51.713
I think a lot of times,
they're just mailing it in,

00:06:51.713 --> 00:06:53.860
they went to some website that says,

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:56.100
'seven best question to
ask during an interview'

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.714
or they have an interview coach,

00:06:57.714 --> 00:06:59.433
or their dad told them to say something.

00:06:59.433 --> 00:07:02.345
I hate mailed in bullshit,
I like when they ask,

00:07:02.345 --> 00:07:03.797
or when they pander to me, like,

00:07:03.797 --> 00:07:05.619
'Gary, what're you gonna
do when you buy the Jets.'

00:07:05.619 --> 00:07:08.080
Like, I don't need that
either, though I like it,

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.402
I like being coddled, I don't need it.

00:07:10.402 --> 00:07:13.885
We're there to try to help
them help me help us help them

00:07:13.885 --> 00:07:17.007
as a team, and the truth
is what I'm looking for,

00:07:17.007 --> 00:07:19.457
so, you know, not that they
have to go the other way

00:07:19.457 --> 00:07:21.524
and ask a hard-hitting question, either.

00:07:21.524 --> 00:07:23.845
I don't care if it's,
if they truly wanna know

00:07:23.845 --> 00:07:25.586
what I'm gonna do when I buy the Jets,

00:07:25.586 --> 00:07:26.759
then I'm happy.

00:07:26.759 --> 00:07:28.477
If they truly wanna ask me, like,

00:07:28.477 --> 00:07:31.298
is the vulnerability of
this company your charisma

00:07:31.298 --> 00:07:33.156
and personal brand of
what happens without you,

00:07:33.156 --> 00:07:35.323
if they truly mean it and
they're not just trying

00:07:35.323 --> 00:07:37.482
to win points on asking
the hard-hitting question,

00:07:38.612 --> 00:07:42.043
you know, I think that that
has always been my thing.

00:07:42.043 --> 00:07:44.644
I don't want anybody going
too far in either direction

00:07:44.644 --> 00:07:46.625
of bullshit, just keep it in the lane of

00:07:46.625 --> 00:07:48.211
something that really matters to you,

00:07:48.211 --> 00:07:49.937
and so, sometimes I like the, you know,

00:07:49.937 --> 00:07:52.074
now it's spurring some memories, 
sometimes I like when they ask

00:07:52.074 --> 00:07:54.548
about, like, how much can they contribute

00:07:54.548 --> 00:07:59.325
to the 401k, or, do
you really, truly allow

00:07:59.325 --> 00:08:01.119
unlimited vacation time, like, like,

00:08:01.119 --> 00:08:02.941
I like when it's just
actually coming from something

00:08:02.941 --> 00:08:04.415
they wanna know for themselves.

00:08:04.415 --> 00:08:06.343
I feel like I'm there to
provide value to them.

00:08:06.343 --> 00:08:09.698
I don't think an interviewee
is trying to sell me

00:08:09.698 --> 00:08:12.065
on them to work for me,

00:08:12.065 --> 00:08:16.244
I think I'm trying to sell
them on working for me.

00:08:16.244 --> 00:08:19.762
And so, I really flip interviews
on their head that way,

00:08:20.602 --> 00:08:21.957
and that's an interesting
thing altogether.

00:08:21.957 --> 00:08:23.280
Maybe I just love selling so much,

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:27.473
maybe I'm trying from the day we meet,

00:08:27.473 --> 00:08:29.653
create an environment of safety,

00:08:29.653 --> 00:08:31.911
which I'm very proud of
that I think I create here.

00:08:33.937 --> 00:08:35.046
That's the answer.

00:08:36.212 --> 00:08:37.865
- [Steve] Cool.

00:08:37.865 --> 00:08:40.659
Video question from Vineyard Paul.

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:42.591
- Vineyard Paul, I like Vineyard Paul.

00:08:46.115 --> 00:08:47.160
- Hey, Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:48.739
on holiday so I've got a question for you.

00:08:48.739 --> 00:08:50.619
What have you learned from WineDeals,

00:08:50.619 --> 00:08:51.536
you Instagram account,

00:08:51.536 --> 00:08:53.475
the best place to buy wine in America?

00:08:53.475 --> 00:08:54.659
Link me up here.

00:08:54.659 --> 00:08:56.853
What have you learnt from you
WineDeals Instagram account

00:08:56.853 --> 00:08:58.154
that small wineries can still use

00:08:58.154 --> 00:08:59.454
to make a buck on Instagram?

00:08:59.454 --> 00:09:00.057
Cheers.

00:09:00.057 --> 00:09:01.195
- Good job, Vineyard Paul.

00:09:01.195 --> 00:09:02.193
- [Paul] Hey, Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:09:02.193 --> 00:09:03.470
on holiday so I've got a question for you.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:04.991
- Oh, that was on loop?

00:09:04.991 --> 00:09:05.676
- [Steve] Yeah.

00:09:05.676 --> 00:09:06.535
- That's cool.

00:09:06.535 --> 00:09:07.696
Does it loop?

00:09:08.936 --> 00:09:10.760
Oh, yeah, yeah, on desktop, I, yeah.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:13.488
Vineyard Paul, great question.

00:09:14.488 --> 00:09:18.552
I've learned what I thought
with creating WineDeals,

00:09:18.552 --> 00:09:20.501
the greatest place on
Instagram to buy wine,

00:09:22.531 --> 00:09:24.715
which is people are
disproportionately paying attention

00:09:24.715 --> 00:09:27.513
to Instagram, plus Instagram has built-in

00:09:27.513 --> 00:09:29.231
word of mouth functionality.

00:09:29.231 --> 00:09:32.191
People, by habit, when they
see something on Instagram

00:09:32.191 --> 00:09:34.269
that they think somebody else is going to

00:09:34.269 --> 00:09:36.533
be interested in, tag their
friends in the comments.

00:09:36.533 --> 00:09:38.738
They leave a comment with
that person's user handle,

00:09:38.738 --> 00:09:41.130
because everybody's paying
attention to Instagram,

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:42.430
they're seeing that alert,

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:44.136
then they are then checking it out.

00:09:44.136 --> 00:09:46.308
I have not seen anything work like this

00:09:46.308 --> 00:09:49.233
since early e-mail, and early Twitter.

00:09:49.233 --> 00:09:51.775
I'm sorry, this is very early
e-mail, very early Twitter,

00:09:51.775 --> 00:09:53.749
where anybody who's on the platform

00:09:53.749 --> 00:09:56.674
is disproportionately paying
attention to the platform,

00:09:56.674 --> 00:10:00.169
in a way that creates
aggressive word of mouth

00:10:00.169 --> 00:10:02.467
infrastructure, so we're
selling a lot of wine,

00:10:03.507 --> 00:10:05.556
we haven't even taken it that serious.

00:10:05.556 --> 00:10:07.355
Steve, you're about to get
really, really in there,

00:10:07.355 --> 00:10:08.794
you know, we're kind of
in the Summer months,

00:10:08.794 --> 00:10:10.733
which is fun, but now, here we are,

00:10:10.733 --> 00:10:12.798
as I start seeing, you know, it's funny,

00:10:12.798 --> 00:10:15.899
when I start seeing leaves go orange and,

00:10:15.899 --> 00:10:18.245
you know, all that stuff,
like, that's when I know

00:10:18.245 --> 00:10:19.706
it's time for money, right,

00:10:19.706 --> 00:10:21.947
because I came from retail,
and the fourth quarter

00:10:21.947 --> 00:10:25.111
is what, I mean, literally, this day.

00:10:25.111 --> 00:10:26.556
I'm gonna literally get goosebumps.

00:10:26.556 --> 00:10:31.304
This exact day for me, the
day I stopped going to school,

00:10:32.384 --> 00:10:34.230
was the greatest day of my life,

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:36.493
because it was the day that shit was about

00:10:36.493 --> 00:10:38.264
to get serious at Wine Library,

00:10:38.264 --> 00:10:39.814
because we're about to do a lot of sales

00:10:39.814 --> 00:10:42.796
in these next 16 to 20
weeks, and football,

00:10:42.796 --> 00:10:44.527
and no school.

00:10:44.527 --> 00:10:46.754
I mean, just like the best.

00:10:46.754 --> 00:10:50.853
So, as much as I love running VaynerMedia,

00:10:50.853 --> 00:10:52.675
and as much as this is gonna
be a much bigger company,

00:10:52.675 --> 00:10:54.069
and as much as this is awesome,

00:10:54.069 --> 00:10:56.472
I'm still a merchant at heart, and I,

00:10:56.472 --> 00:11:00.605
as, I'm fired up to,
you know, here, today,

00:11:00.605 --> 00:11:03.310
and ready to go, but I can't lie,

